sara_sales: Hey team — ACME demo today hit a streaming issue. During a long generated reply the client got a partial JSON and then the SSE connection closed. Demo contact says it happened ~1m into the response. Can we triage quickly?

acme_demo_bot: (system) client id: acme-ctx-77, request id: req_0b3f, path: /v1/generate/stream

tariq_se: Can you paste the raw chunk frames the client received? usually we want to see boundary markers and whether the final "\n\n" for the SSE event was delivered.

acme_demo_bot: got it, sending snippet: ```
data: {"id":"tok_1","text":"Hello there, here's a big answer that gets streamed in pieces..."}

data: {"id":"tok_1","text":"...continuation part 2 (truncated)\n"}
```

mike_runtime: Looks like the last event was cut mid-line — the client shows no trailing double newline that terminates an SSE event. Server logs show a remote reset: `write ECONNRESET` from proxy IP 10.4.21.9 around same time. Could be proxy buffering + connection reuse.

jenna_support: @mike_runtime do we see any rate-limit or connection limit on the dedicated pool? ACME on dedicated dev slot with 2 workers.

mike_runtime: No rate-limit. Worker accepted the request and started streaming. After ~60s there was a socket close from the proxy. This screams at nginx proxy_buffering or an intermediate load balancer killing idle connections. Are they routing through a CDN?

tariq_se: Customer uses a lightweight edge proxy inside their infra (envoy-based). Envoy has a downstream idle timeout default = 1m in some configs. If the model is streaming but occasionally stalls longer than that, envoy may close the connection mid-event. Two quick reproductions: 1) add keepalive pings (empty comment lines) 2) ensure SSE event flush frequency < timeout.

sara_sales: Can you show example keepalive ping format we can ask them to add? The demo app is a vanilla web client using EventSource.

tariq_se: Use SSE comment ping every 15s like: ```
: ping

```
EventSource ignores lines that start with `:` but that keeps the connection active. Another option is to force smaller chunks from the server — flush every ~8-16KB or every 5-10s.

jenna_support: Also check their proxy config: for nginx add `proxy_buffering off; proxy_buffers 4 256k; proxy_busy_buffers_size 256k;` and `proxy_read_timeout 300s;` for streaming endpoints. For envoy check `stream_idle_timeout` and `max_connection_duration`.

acme_demo_bot: Customer says they have Cloudflare in front of their origin for the demo. Cloudflare by default buffers responses for non-websocket long polling. That could re-chunk or drop SSE.

mike_runtime: Cloudflare is known to buffer SSE unless you enable `disable_chunked_encoding` or use Cloudflare Workers with passthrough. For a quick test ask them to curl the endpoint from inside their network and capture raw bytes: `curl -v -N "https://.../v1/generate/stream"` (-N disables buffering in curl). If curl shows intact SSE frames then the issue is the CDN.

sara_sales: I'll DM them the exact curl + keepalive guidance. Any server-side quick mitigation from our side?

mike_runtime: We can add periodic `event: keepalive
data: {}

` pushes from the runtime on long responses. Low-impact and helps proxies detect activity. I'll spin a canary that emits keepalive every 12s for streaming routes for the dedicated pool.

tariq_se: Also note client JSON parsing: EventSource emits `data:` chunks; the client concatenates until it sees an empty line. If they attempt to JSON.parse partial data between `data:` events they will error. Our docs should emphasize assembling multi-line `data:` events before parsing.

acme_demo_bot: Customer replied — they can temporarily bypass Cloudflare and confirm. If bypass fixes it, we'll call out Cloudflare as the root cause for the demo.

sara_sales: Awesome. Can we get an ETA for the canary? I'm on the call in 30m.

mike_runtime: Give me 20m. I'll enable keepalive canary for the dedicated dev slot and post the canary endpoint here.

tariq_se: I'll add a snippet for the client-side safe parser to avoid throwing on partial frames: ```
let buf = '' ;
source.onmessage = e => {
  buf += e.data;
  try {
    const obj = JSON.parse(buf);
    buf = '' ;
    handle(obj);
  } catch (err) {
    // wait for more chunks
  }
};
```

sara_sales: Thanks all — I'll update ACME and loop back on the call. :thumbsup:
